schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Delegation and Representation in DAOs
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A design guide for DAO delegation systems, covering four delegation types
    (full, topic-based, liquid, time-limited), key design questions, concrete
    recommendations for local nodes (optional delegation, power caps of
    20-30%, transparency, accountability, always-available direct voting),
    accountability mechanisms (delegate reports, recall, term limits, conflict
    disclosure), and common mistakes to avoid.
  audience: DAO-Operator / Local-Node governance designers
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      reducing-voter-fatigue-via-delegation vs concentrating-power-in-delegates
      — the whole article is an argument for building delegation carefully so
      it solves the first problem without causing the second.
    - >-
      flexibility-of-liquid-delegation vs complexity-requiring-sophisticated-
      tooling.
  related_concepts:
    - liquid-democracy
    - delegation-accountability
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/delegation-representation.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-6675a98b235f
  source_lineage: delegation-representation.md
  notes: >
    # assessed-but-excluded

    - No named tools/platforms are cited in this article (unlike dao-tooling.md
      or dao-governance-models.md) — no resource objects created; this is a
      pure governance-design guide with generic "on-chain/off-chain/hybrid"
      language rather than named implementations.
    - No people/community profiles, exact locations, or high-risk triggers
      identified. Note: the article does make a governance-design
      recommendation (cap delegate power at 20-30%) — this is generic best-
      practice advice, not a claim about a specific real DAO's funding/legal
      status, so it was not treated as a high-risk governance recommendation
      requiring a public-use-boundary; flagged here for reviewer judgment call.
